  "version": 1,
  "questionId": "q16",
  "speechText": "For me, I start with least privilege. The agent should only have the tools it really needs. The main reason is that the model should not have direct access to internal services or databases. For example, before a tool runs, the backend checks the arguments, permission, and access scope. The model can suggest an action, but the backend makes the final decision. For sensitive actions, I would also require user confirmation when needed. At the same time, I keep safe logs with IDs and avoid sensitive data. For write actions, I use idempotency so retries do not create duplicates. So overall, the LLM can suggest, but the backend controls access, execution, and safety.",
